MKDIR                                                              MKDIR

NAME
    mkdir - make directories, Unix-style

FORMAT
    mkdir [-p] DIR ...

TEMPLATE
    none - flags are hand-parsed Unix bundles; `mkdir ?` prints
    usage

PATH
    C:mkdir

FUNCTION
    Creates directories - several at once if asked. Without -p each
    DIR is created directly: its parent must already exist, and an
    existing DIR is an error, like Unix mkdir.

    With -p every missing directory along the path is created in
    turn - `mkdir -p work:a/b/c` makes a, then a/b, then a/b/c -
    and an already-existing directory is NOT an error (the whole
    point of -p). A path component that already exists as a FILE is
    an error, reported at the component actually in the way.
    Device/volume names (the part before ':') are never created,
    only descended into.

    DIR names are literal - no pattern expansion. `mkdir "#?"`
    makes a directory called #?, not one per match. (The shell
    doesn't expand patterns either; on AmigaDOS each command
    matches its own, and mkdir has nothing to match against.)

OPTIONS
    -p  create missing parent directories; an existing directory
        is not an error

RESULTS
    Return code 0 when clean, 10 when a directory failed, 20 on
    Ctrl-C. Errors on one DIR are reported and the rest still run.
    More than 32 arguments is an error, never a silent truncation.

EXAMPLES
    mkdir work:newdir
    mkdir a b c
    mkdir -p work:a/b/c
    mkdir -p ram:deep/nested/dir

SEE ALSO
    ls.doc, cp.doc, mv.doc, rm.doc, tools-audit.md
